      "narrative": "Maricopa County’s residential market is mostly flat to slightly rising on prices, with Redfin reporting a median sale price of $484,000 over the three months ending May 2026, down 0.8% year over year, while Phoenix Agent Magazine shows a 1.9% annual gain to a $514,593 median in June.[1][2] Inventory is tight but within balanced levels, with months of supply around 3.7-3.8 and active listings down modestly from last year, supporting neither a clear buyer’s nor seller’s market.[2][3][4] Buyer-seller dynamics are balanced and slightly tilting toward buyers, as days on market have risen into the 44-73 day range and a large share of listings cut prices, yet constrained new listings and solid contract activity keep sellers from having to deeply discount.[1][2][3][9] Locally, the market is supported by ongoing in-migration into the Phoenix area, substantial new construction (including tens of thousands of planned homes in the city of Maricopa), and a diverse employer base that helps sustain housing demand even as higher mortgage rates cap budgets.[5][7][9]",
