      "narrative": "Pinal County’s residential market is essentially flat on prices: the median sale price was $383,849 in May 2026, down 0.3% year over year, indicating a mild post-boom correction rather than renewed appreciation.[5] Inventory and demand generally track the broader Greater Phoenix pattern, where supply sits only slightly above balanced while demand is soft, creating a buyer-leaning but not oversupplied environment with common seller concessions and negotiation room.[4][8] Migration from metro Phoenix and master-planned growth are major drivers, as Pinal is one of the state’s key population-growth areas and a spillover destination for households pushed outward by affordability and new construction patterns.[7] On the economic side, Pinal benefits from the expanding semiconductor and logistics corridor, including recent approval of a 400 MW battery storage project, which reinforces long-term employer and infrastructure demand rather than near-term speculative froth.[1]",
